// TEST: Idx Card Unbounded Field (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// The second v1 fence condition, and the one that separates a constrained
// RECORD from an index TYPE. `n` is an honest `Int` field — perfectly legal on
// an ordinary struct, and the conjunct even constrains it — but an unbounded
// field has no box, so there is nothing to enumerate:
//
//   struct Unbounded, field 'n': unbounded field — an index-eligible struct
//   needs a static min and max
//
// Note what is NOT accepted as a substitute for a bound: the where-conjunct
// `n < 10` below bounds `n` from above in every sense a reader cares about,
// and the fence still refuses. That is deliberate. Conjuncts are the FILTER
// over the box; they are not consulted to DISCOVER the box, because doing so
// would mean solving the constraints to find the iteration range — the exact
// solve-don't-assert inversion the design refuses. The box comes from the
// field bounds and only from the field bounds; the conjuncts then exclude.
//
// The plan does leave a door open here, as an efficiency deferral rather than
// a semantics gap: same-record dependent bounds (`l_out: min=abs(l1-l2)`) are
// out of the box GRAMMAR but desugar to conjuncts given absolute bounds, and
// tightening the heads from them is deferral §8.5. index-types/152 is the
// negative control for that boundary as it stands today.
// ============================================================================
static struct Unbounded {
    n: Int
} where n < 10

let static CARD = idx_card(Unbounded)
